<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1478">1478</a>–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">House of Tudor</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a>, 3rd Duke of Buckingham, was one of the greatest English nobles of the early Tudor period, a descendant of Edward III with large estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welsh-marches">Welsh Marches</a>, including Brecknock. His royal blood and hostility to Cardinal Wolsey made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> suspicious, and he was convicted of treason and beheaded in 1521, after which his lands were forfeited to the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-stafford-3rd-duke-of-buckingham.jpg" alt="Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Stafford_3rd_Duke_of_Buckingham_1520.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> was born on 3 February 1478 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecon-castle">Brecon Castle</a>, the eldest son of Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham, and Katherine Woodville, which made him a nephew of Elizabeth Woodville and Edward IV. In October 1483 his father rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> and was beheaded without trial on 2 November, his honours forfeited. The boy is said to have been hidden in houses in Herefordshire. After Henry VII's victory at Bosworth he was made a Knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> as Duke of Buckingham in October 1485, and his wardship passed to the king's mother, Margaret Beaufort, in whose households he was probably educated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Buckingham became a Knight of the Garter in 1495 and in 1497 was a captain in the forces sent against the rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. At the wedding of Arthur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, and Katherine of Aragon in 1501 he is said to have worn a gown worth £1500. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s coronation in 1509 he served as Lord High Constable, an office he claimed by hereditary right, and as Lord High Steward, and he joined the Privy Council. He rebuilt his manor of Thornbury, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, as a towered castle after receiving a licence to crenellate in 1510.</p><p>In 1513 he commanded 500 men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in 1520 he attended the Field of the Cloth of Gold with his wife, Eleanor, a daughter of Henry Percy, 4th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. Charged with keeping order in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">the Marches</a>, he was rebuked by the king in 1518, and he was never part of the king's inner circle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> and linked to much of the upper aristocracy, Buckingham drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s suspicion. In 1520 the king ordered Cardinal Wolsey to watch him and examined witnesses himself. Summoned to court in April 1521, the duke was sent to the Tower and tried before 17 peers on charges of listening to prophecies of the king's death and intending to kill him. He was executed on Tower Hill on 17 May 1521 and attainted by Act of Parliament in 1523. Some conclude he was almost certainly guilty, but Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a> complained that the key evidence came from servants threatened and tortured into false confessions. His fall is depicted in Shakespeare's play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edward Stafford</b>, Duke of Buckingham, was executed in 1521 and the lordship came to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Lordship of Brecknock</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecon-castle">Brecon Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Tower Hill, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.95,-3.39&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Brecon Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4473 368 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4923 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4923 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.95,-3.39&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
